<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#galician-portuguese">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galician-Portuguese</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Old Galician</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galician-Portuguese was the Romance language of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and northern Portugal, from which modern Galician and Portuguese developed. From the late 12th to the mid-14th century it was the main language of lyric poetry across the Iberian kingdoms, used by troubadours and by Alfonso X in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantigas-de-santa-maria">Cantigas de Santa Maria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galician-portuguese.jpg" alt="Galician-Portuguese" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fobos92 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Idioma_galaicoportugu%C3%A9s.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Galician-Portuguese developed in the region of the former Roman province of Gallaecia from the Vulgar Latin brought by Roman soldiers, colonists and magistrates. Centuries of contact, after a period of bilingualism, extinguished the native languages, though Gallaecian and Lusitanian influences can be detected in some words and in place names. The first known phonetic changes began under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a> (411 to 585) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> (585 to 711), and by the year 800 it had become the vernacular of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>. It was first spoken between the Atlantic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro River</a> in the south, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> later carried it south of the Douro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Documents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> from the 9th century contain Galician-Portuguese words, among them the Doação à Igreja de Sozello of 870. Among documents in the language itself, the Testament of Afonso II, dated 27 June 1214, is most certainly Galician-Portuguese, and the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> is probably a document from the monastery of Melón dated to 1231. The earliest poetic texts date from about 1195 to about 1225.</p><p>The language was used for literature from the last years of the 12th century to about the middle of the 14th century and was almost always the only language of lyric poetry in the Christian kingdoms of the peninsula. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-of-portugal">Dinis of Portugal</a> made it official in Portugal in 1290; until then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> had been the written language of royal documents.</p><p>The language lost its unity when the County of Portugal separated from the Kingdom of Leon to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal</a>, and the two versions diverged. Portuguese spread south during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> and replaced Arabic and other Romance languages. Galician was the main written language in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> until the 16th century, when Castilian Spanish displaced it, and a literature in Galician re-emerged in the 19th century during the Rexurdimento.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>About 160 poets are recorded, among them Bernal de Bonaval, Pero da Ponte and Johan Garcia de Guilhade. Nearly 1,700 poems survive in the three main secular genres: the cantigas d'amor, the male-voiced love lyric, the cantigas d'amigo, the female-voiced love lyric, and the cantigas d'escarnho e de mal dizer, which range from personal invective to satire. King Dinis left 137 texts, more than any other author. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a> wrote his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantigas-de-santa-maria">Cantigas de Santa Maria</a>, over 400 poems about miracles of the Virgin, in Galician-Portuguese although he used Castilian for prose. The language is the ancestor of modern Galician, Portuguese, Fala and Eonavian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Galician–Portuguese” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Galician-Portuguese</b>, which contemporaries held to be one language, flourished in the 13th and 14th centuries, and some 2,000 cantigas survive in its love songs, songs of a woman for her beloved, songs of scorn and religious songs; Alfonso X the Wise was among its patrons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-galicia">Kingdom of Galicia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cantigas de Santa Maria (&quot;Canticles of Holy Mary&quot;) are 420 poems with musical notation, written in medieval <b>Galician-Portuguese</b> at the court of Alfonso X of Castile, called El Sabio, &quot;the Wise&quot; (1221-1284).</blockquote><a href="/songs/cantigas-de-santa-maria">Cantigas de Santa Maria (Canticles of Holy Mary)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Galician-Portuguese</b> became a major literary language, used for the troubadour lyric and the Cantigas de Santa Maria.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia">Kingdom of Galicia</a></li></ul></section>
